1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Here https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/L%C3%BCders-263
2 CONT
2 CONT Lyders, Frederick William , Builder and Contractor, 169 Queen Street, North Dunedin. Mr. Lyders was born and educated in Denmark and arrived at Wellington in the year 1874. After working for a time at log-cutting for a large sawmi
2 CONC ll in the North Island he met with a serious accident to his right leg, which necessitated his remaining in the Wellington Hospital for about ten months. He then came to Dunedin and started in business as a building contractor. Am
2 CONC ong the many buildings erected by him are the Children's Ward and kitchen of the Dunedin Hospital, the Manse Street portion of Wain's Hotel, Hayward's warehouse, and Aldred's Buildings in George Street, the Mornington, Palmerston,
2 CONC  and several other public schools, residences for the Otago Education Board, and a third storey to Dalgety's large grain warehouse, which comprises a whole block of buildings in itself. Mr. Lyders was married about twelve years ag
2 CONC o to Miss M. Smith, of Waikato, a daughter of Captain Smith, of the Imperial Army, and has had a family of seven children, six of whom are alive. Of recent years Mr. Lyders has been a very successful land speculator, having acquir
2 CONC ed several blocks of land around Dunedin and cut them up into building allotments, which have been readily sought after by the general public.
2 CONT
2 CONT From here: https://cargillscastle.co.nz/history
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1892, despite original claims that the concrete construction would render the Castle fireproof, a large fire gutted the interior of the house. The fire started in an outhouse and spread to the main building through a connecting
2 CONC  wooden beam and destroyed the original lavish interior woodwork.
2 CONT
2 CONT It took almost three years to restore the Castle, and the opportunity was used to add a ballroom to the eastern side. The Cargill family owned and resided in the Castle until 1908 when it was purchased by the Lyders who lived ther
2 CONC e until 1923.
2 CONT
2 CONT From here https://juxtaposenz.blogspot.com/2018/04/cargills-castle-dunedin-new-zealand.html
2 CONT
2 CONT As a child, I spent many happy hours with my Grandmother, listening to stories of her extended family, who settled in Dunedin after arriving from Denmark. Many of the stories revolved around time spent with cousins at 'The Cliffs'
2 CONC  or as it's known locally, 'Cargill's Castle'. Her uncle - Harry Lyders - built the 'castle' for Edward Cargill, subsequently purchasing the property early in the 20th century for his own family. One story in particular has stuck 
2 CONC to this day - how as children, she and her cousins were allowed to use the ballroom as an indoor rollerskating rink! The ballroom was added some years after the castle's original construction, which Uncle Harry considered a "folly
2 CONC ", and consequently had no desire to preserve. A holt was put on indoor cricket matches however, after windows were smashed and needed replacing to keep the weather out.
