1 NAME Ove Chr /Due/
2 GIVN Ove Chr
2 SURN Due
2 _MARNM Due
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Australian National Memorial, Villers-Bretonneux
2 ADDR
3 CTRY France
1 BIRT
2 DATE 19 APR 1882
2 ADDR
3 STAE Hedmark
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE 3 SEP 1916
2 ADDR
3 CTRY France
1 OCCU Dampskipskaptein, militær
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Bodde i ca 17 år i Vest Australia, og kom i 1916 med en australsk infanteribataljon til Vestfronten. Falt i krigen 35 år gammel, meldt savnet siden høsten 1917
2 CONT
2 CONT _________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian DUE 
2 CONT
2 CONT Regimental number 3757  Religion Lutheran  Occupation Sailor  Address Gerladton, Western Australia  Marital status Single  Age at embarkation 31  Next of kin Father, Ove Gude Due, Christiania, Norway  Enlistment date 17 September 
2 CONC 1915  Rank on enlistment Private  Unit name 28th Battalion, 9th Reinforcement  AWM Embarkation Roll number 23/45/3  Embarkation details Unit embarked from Fremantle, Western Australia, on board HMAT A28 Miltiades on 12 February 19
2 CONC 16  Rank from Nominal Roll Private  Unit from Nominal Roll 51st Battalion  Fate Killed in Action 3 September 1916  Place of burial No known grave  Commemoration details Australian National Memorial, Villers-Bretonneux, France Vill
2 CONC ers-Bretonneux is a village about 15 km east of Amiens. The Memorial stands on the high ground ('Hill 104') behind the Villers-Bretonneux Military Cemetery, Fouilloy, which is about 2 km north of Villers-Bretonneux on the east sid
2 CONC e of the road to Fouilloy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Australian National Memorial, Villers-Bretonneux is approached through the Military Cemetery, at the end of which is an open grass lawn which leads into a three-sided court. The two pavilions on the left and right are linked b
2 CONC y the north and south walls to the back (east) wall, from which rises the focal point of the Memorial, a 105 foot tall tower, of fine ashlar. A staircase leads to an observation platform, 64 feet above the ground, from which furth
2 CONC er staircases lead to an observation room. This room contains a circular stone tablet with bronze pointers indicating the Somme villages whose names have become synonymous with battles of the Great War; other battle fields in Fran
2 CONC ce and Belgium in which Australians fought; and far beyond, Gallipoli and Canberra. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On the three walls, which are faced with Portland stone, are the names of 10,885 Australians who were killed in France and who have no known grave. The 'blocking course' above them bears the names of the Australian Battle Honours.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war an appeal in Australia raised £22,700, of which £12,500 came from Victorian school children, with the request that the majority of the funds be used to build a new school in Villers-Bretonneux. The boys' school opene
2 CONC d in May 1927, and contains an inscription stating that the school was the gift of Victorian schoolchildren, twelve hundred of whose fathers are buried in the Villers-Bretonneux cemetery, with the names of many more recorded on th
2 CONC e Memorial. Villers-Bretonneux is now twinned with Robinvale, Victoria, which has in its main square a memorial to the links between the two towns. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Panel number, Roll of Honour,
2 CONT   Australian War Memorial 152  Miscellaneous information from
2 CONT   cemetery records Town. Geraldton, Western Australia  Other details War service: Western Front
2 CONT
2 CONT Medals: British War Medal, Victory Medal 
