1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In an article in “Damebladet” (Lady’s Magazine) of April 9, 1939 called Working housewife and mother to ten children Mrs. Valborg Birkeland tells us a little about her experience as housewife and mother to a large 
2 CONC crowd of children.  “Yes, You know I have traditions to honor, because I am a direct descendent of Tordenskiold and in the song about him it states in one of the verses “daughters six and sons twelve”.  I am from a family of twelv
2 CONC e children myself, so it is in my blood.  There is no such wealth in this world as a large family of children.  It creates a life and a happiness that cannot be compared to anything else.”  She continues:  “When my husband lost hi
2 CONC s position as City District Physician as a result of the abolishment of such positions, I was already established in my little institute for sunlamp treatment and diathermy, so we could nevertheless anticipate a fairly carefree fu
2 CONC ture.  I have been working with this institute for fifteen years now and it is still our best economical support.  However, I have thoroughly experienced what it means to be both a working woman and a housewife.  Very often I have
2 CONC  had to work twelve hours in the institute, not even being able to get home for dinner.  Otherwise, I would say that the work one does for home and children is a work of joy that one never regrets.”  The article contained picture 
2 CONC of Mrs. Doctor Birkeland as a keen garden grower showing her husband how well it grows and how nice it looks.  There was also a picture of herself and her nine children as well as three pairs of twins from the youngest generation.
