1 NAME William George Ainslie /Kennedy/
2 GIVN William George Ainslie
2 _MARNM Kennedy
1 SEX M
1 OCCU Commander
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Northern Patrol and its 10th Squadron played a major role in preventing contraband leaving or entering German-controlled territory in 1914 - 1917. The squadron ships were old light cruisers that had to be withd
2 CONC rawn in the winter of 1914 because of the damage suffered from the rough seas. Their role was taken over by converted liners and passenger/cargo liners - the armed merchant cruisers or AMC's. 
2 CONT Royal Navy Commander W. G. A. Kennedy was in charge of the armed merchant cruiser HMS India, a converted P&O liner. 
2 CONT Naval History Net describes what happened on August 8, 1915 when HMS India was sunk by the German submarine U-22:  
2 CONT "The commanding officer of India was Commander W. G. A. Kennedy, R.N., and he was very busy during Sunday patrolling off the West Fjord through a line which may be described with accuracy as along the parallel of 67°30' N., from a
2 CONC s far west as 11°30' E. to as far east as 13°30' E., which brought him very close to the Norwegian coast. The armed trawlers Saxon and Newland were also hereabouts." --
2 CONT "The summer afternoon began to wane, a steamer of sorts was reported coming up from the south-east, and Commander Kennedy altered course to try to intercept. He had just left the bridge temporarily to give the wireless operator an
2 CONC  order, leaving Sub-Lieut. E. W. Alltree, R.N.R., in charge, the time being 5.40 p.m. Suddenly the look-out man on the fo'c'sle reported : "Submarine on starboard bow!" Looking in the direction indicated, Alltree saw the white fea
2 CONC thery track of a torpedo approaching from only 300 yards away. Commander Kennedy had barely left the wireless-house and reached the next (hurricane) deck, when he heard the alarm gongs sounding.
2 CONT Running back to the bridge, he was in time to witness the torpedo track foaming at an angle of about 30 degrees from the bow.
2 CONT "Full speed ahead! Hard aport!"
2 CONT The order was obeyed promptly, and at first it looked as if danger had just been averted, for the track seemed to have reached the ship and nothing to have happened. Evidently the missile had gone under the hull!
2 CONT Then came that ominous thud, with the explosion. A hit!
2 CONT The torpedo struck its target below No. 3 starboard gun, and immediately the ex-P. & O. liner began to sink by the stern, heeling 10 degrees to port. The Commander ordered messages requesting assistance to be sent out, and then ca
2 CONC me, "Abandon Ship!" --
2 CONT "The result was that, notwithstanding all the efforts to save life, there perished nine officers and 107 men, only 189 officers and men surviving, but 157 of these had either dived off the ship or, having gone down with her, came 
2 CONC up again."
2 CONT "Commander Kennedy had neither the time nor the chance to readapt and react to the quickly changing conditions. Five minutes before he was on the bridge; then he was trying to save his men; another swift spasm and he found himself
2 CONC  going down with the India, clinging to the foremost port davit, till he eventually floated up alive among the wreckage and was hauled into a boat."--
2 CONT "With full honours, and attended by Norwegian naval, military, and civil authorities, the sad funeral ceremonies took place, flowers, evergreens, and wreaths from the kindly Norwegians being placed with the White Ensign, Union Jac
2 CONC k, and Red Ensign picked up from the India." --
2 CONT "But now began a dismal period for eager warriors snatched out of war and death into inactivity. No more chance of toiling for victory against the enemy; no more days and nights on patrol. They were transferred to an internment ca
2 CONC mp near Lillehammer (Jørstadmoen), and three long years in a strange land must pass before the War should finish."
2 CONT (https://naval-history.net/WW1Memoir-10CSNPatrol.htm)
2 CONT As an officer Kennedy was allowed to live outside of the camp and he was free to travel within the country. 
2 CONT On May 2, 1916, Oslo newspapers reported that commander W.G.A. Kennedy and Miss Fedora Lundh had been married in the city´s English church. They were presented with a platter engraved with the signatures of all the men in the Jørs
2 CONC tadmoen camp.
