1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Ataata (Qaviaraq dialect for 'father')
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/27431123/clifford-s_-topkok
2 CONT
2 CONT Anchorage Daily News July 26, 1992
2 CONT
2 CONT Lifelong Alaskan Clifford S. "Babe" Topkok, 53, died July 18 at his Anchorage home. Visitation was at Witzleben's Bragaw Chapel. A service was held at Central Lutheran Church with Chaplain John Maakstead and the Rev. Eric Ottum of
2 CONC ficiating. His sons and nephew, Jerry Topkok, were pallbearers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Topkok was born Aug. 2, 1938, near Mary's Igloo. He attended Mount Edgecumbe High School and completed his graduate equivalent diploma in Anchorage. He served in the U.S. Army from 1957 to 1959 and from 1962 to 1964. He survey
2 CONC ed for the new Anchorage International Airport, worked highway construction at Teller and near Copper Center, worked at Prudhoe Bay and was an excellent cook who worked at many fine Anchorage restaurants, according to his family. 
2 CONC Through Mr. Topkok's artwork, many people enjoyed Alaska's rich Native culture. He carved baleen, whale and mastodon ivory, made sketches and wrote poetry. He enjoyed sharing his artistry and teaching his techniques to others. He 
2 CONC volunteered time at many local schools. His work is displayed in homes and shops worldwide. He was active in Native ministries, Native outreach, and helping others to accomplish the sobriety and spirituality that he had successful
2 CONC ly vowed to observe more than six years ago. Through his influence in the Teller Native Corp., he helped secure grants and funds for construction projects and community improvement. He helped organize the Association for Stranded 
2 CONC Rural Alaskans. Mr. Topkok enjoyed Alaska's beauty and solidarity, hiking, camping, and especially fishing. He spent much of his spare time salmon fishing on the Kenai Peninsula. He also enjoyed playing his guitar and singing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Topkok is survived by his wife, Aileen; his sons, Brian D., C. Scott, C. Sean, X. Dale, all of Anchorage, and Jeffrey of California; his granddaughter, Trista; his mother, Gussie, both of Anchorage; his brothers, Alfred and Wa
2 CONC yne of Teller, and Chester and Edward of Anchorage; his sister, Sadie Kakaruk of Anchorage; and Bonnie Mencer; and many friends and relatives. Memorial donations may be sent to a scholarship fund for Trista Topkok, c| o P.O. Box 1
2 CONC 40975, Anchorage 99514.
