1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Aqituaq means "No Name". I know that his Eskimo name meant "No Name", and Aunt Sadie Kakaruk said that that would be his Eskimo name. The name Aqituaq was given to Joseph Topkok, son of Sean and Amy Topkok. [Sean]
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Iñupiaq Name - Aqituaq
2 CONT
2 CONT Anchorage Daily News July 24, 2004
2 CONT
2 CONT Longtime Anchorage resident Clinton "Scott" Topkok, 38, died suddenly July 21, 2004, at Alaska Native Medical Center. A memorial service was at the Alaska Native Lutheran Church, 15th Avenue and Cordova Street.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Topkok was born April 9, 1966, in Anchorage. He lived in Anchorage all his life until recently moving to Texas. He was a 1984 West High graduate who also had attended the University of Alaska Anchorage. He was currently a ware
2 CONC house technician for the Arctic Slope Regional Corp., but had worked in many businesses in Anchorage before that, including Pepsi Corp., Mapco, Center for Disease Control and Union 76. His family said: "Scott had a wonderful sense
2 CONC  of humor and a kind heart. He loved the outdoors, camping and fishing. He enjoyed taking his family and friends fishing on his boat. He was well loved by everyone. "Scott was always more than willing to lend a helping hand. He ma
2 CONC de an effort to spend quality time with his daughter, family and friends. He touched a lot of hearts, making lifelong friends. He will be sorely missed."
2 CONT
2 CONT He is survived by his daughter, Kaelyn Topkok, mother of his daughter, Tracy Burk; brother and nieces, Jeff, Trista and Lily Topkok; brother, sister-in-law and nephews, Sean, Amy, Christopher and Aaron Topkok; brother and sister-i
2 CONC n-law, Xavier Topkok and Kris Rhoades; and many relatives and friends. He was preceded in death by his parents, Clifford and Aileen Topkok, and brother, Brian Topkok. Arrangements are with Witzleben Family Funeral Home.
